            <ArticleTitle>The value of gamma glutamyltransferase in predicting myocardial infarction in patients with acute coronary syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>37-45</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/fca-2017-0033</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the utility of gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) and C-reactive protein (CRP) in predicting troponin elevation in patients with acute coronary syndrome.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The total of 119 patients were divided into troponin-positive (n = 61) and troponin-negative (n = 58) patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">CRP cut-off value ≥13.4 mg/l had the sensitivity of 68.1% and specificity of 62.5%, while the GGT cut-off value ≥61.5 IU/l had the sensitivity of 66.0% and specificity of 62.0% and combined use of both CRP and GGT had 71.4% sensitivity and 69.6% specificity in predicting troponin increase in acute coronary syndrome patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">GGT might be used as an adjuvant marker for risk assessment patients who present with chest pain and are suspected to have acute coronary syndrome.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
